{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2846 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "ba08c0991d00c97889d890551d36f749f31ae5a94e8a6066eb09757d9ba9bfb2" :edb/db-before-t 2845 :edb/db-after-t 2846 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536158 50 #inst "2026-10-09T17:26:42.502Z" 13194139536158 true] [17592186124490 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386524 13194139536158 true] [17592186129145 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386536 13194139536158 true] [17592186133799 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386521 13194139536158 true] [17592186157234 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386533 13194139536158 true] [17592186166796 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386518 13194139536158 true] [17592186166816 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386530 13194139536158 true] [17592186179575 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386527 13194139536158 true] [17592186386518 1017 #edb/ref 17592186386519 13194139536158 true] [17592186386518 1089 "q-168600" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386518 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536158 true] [17592186386518 1100 "An object generates an electric field given by $F(x,y)=(a{y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j$ in a planar coordinate system, where $a$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nGiven that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386518 1113 #uuid "27df1d8e-6834-5e44-8deb-08daa1d8936f" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386518 1145 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n>$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor our vector field $F,$ the domain is $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=a{y}^{3}-1,\\,Q=3x{y}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3a{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a{y}^{2} & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3a & =3 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1.$" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386519 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386520 13194139536158 true] [17592186386519 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536158 true] [17592186386519 1041 "field-1" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386519 1104 #edb/ref 17592186386520 13194139536158 true] [17592186386519 1110 #uuid "f38f1e39-5f1c-5ba3-aa97-533d7c2e2a78" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386520 1034 "1" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386520 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536158 true] [17592186386520 1111 #uuid "3640e961-2087-5dbc-888b-ece2017017af" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386521 1017 #edb/ref 17592186386522 13194139536158 true] [17592186386521 1089 "q-168601" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386521 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536158 true] [17592186386521 1100 "An object generates an electric field given by $F(x,y)=(a{y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j$ in a planar coordinate system, where $a$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nGiven that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386521 1113 #uuid "27a34945-3d5e-591b-b312-7a451d23daf5" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386521 1145 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn part 1, we found that $a=1.$ So, \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=({y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x{y}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =0+6xy \\\\  & =6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386522 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386523 13194139536158 true] [17592186386522 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536158 true] [17592186386522 1041 "field-1" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386522 1104 #edb/ref 17592186386523 13194139536158 true] [17592186386522 1110 #uuid "26d2c75f-33a1-55f4-a64e-4ec3a4e9a37a" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386523 1034 "6xy" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386523 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536158 true] [17592186386523 1111 #uuid "d7079b7e-7006-5ae0-b1a5-561ded9bd575" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386524 1017 #edb/ref 17592186386525 13194139536158 true] [17592186386524 1089 "q-168602" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386524 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536158 true] [17592186386524 1100 "An object generates an electric field given by $F(x,y)=(a{y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j$ in a planar coordinate system, where $a$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nGiven that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $6xy$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x,y)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $+\\,C,$\nwhere $C$ is a constant-" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386524 1113 #uuid "471f5b43-cf20-52a4-816d-05056195d422" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386524 1145 "In part 1, we found that $a=1.$ So, \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=({y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j.\n$$\n\nThe gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{3}-1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3x{y}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({y}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{y}^{3}-x+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3x{y}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3x{y}^{2}+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{y}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y) & =3x{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =1 \\\\ g(y) & =y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-x+y+C.\n$$" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386525 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386526 13194139536158 true] [17592186386525 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536158 true] [17592186386525 1041 "field-1" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386525 1104 #edb/ref 17592186386526 13194139536158 true] [17592186386525 1110 #uuid "9206c057-5c30-5908-b130-64e08684e928" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386526 1034 "xy^3-x+y" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386526 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536158 true] [17592186386526 1111 #uuid "fa0cf1ec-c924-5648-b786-8759da2b35ca" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386527 1017 #edb/ref 17592186386528 13194139536158 true] [17592186386527 1089 "q-168603" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386527 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536158 true] [17592186386527 1100 "An object generates an electric field given by $F(x,y)=(a{y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j$ in a planar coordinate system, where $a$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nGiven that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $6xy$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x,y)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $xy^3-x+y$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $+\\,C,$\nwhere $C$ is a constant-\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/assets/642a546fc498dfb0f01c0729959f9f68b8dc2d142d966673f204eda910f19211.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Consider the square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ shown above. The flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}^{}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}s.$" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386527 1113 #uuid "506c20c3-e02c-551d-91b9-182a5911222c" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386527 1145 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{1}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=i.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/assets/1939a274191b89f69e51a929654489ed9038df0313f6e47b15e0ff55ad813066.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(({y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}{y}^{3}-1\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386528 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386529 13194139536158 true] [17592186386528 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536158 true] [17592186386528 1041 "field-1" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386528 1104 #edb/ref 17592186386529 13194139536158 true] [17592186386528 1110 #uuid "b244c666-8283-5c23-9e57-75f005a0fe77" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386529 1034 "y^3-1" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386529 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536158 true] [17592186386529 1111 #uuid "34c117c1-08bd-5c89-95ee-4ff1d579f0c1" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386530 1017 #edb/ref 17592186386531 13194139536158 true] [17592186386530 1089 "q-168604" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386530 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536158 true] [17592186386530 1100 "An object generates an electric field given by $F(x,y)=(a{y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j$ in a planar coordinate system, where $a$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nGiven that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $6xy$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x,y)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $xy^3-x+y$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $+\\,C,$\nwhere $C$ is a constant-\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/assets/642a546fc498dfb0f01c0729959f9f68b8dc2d142d966673f204eda910f19211.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Consider the square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ shown above. The flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}^{}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $y^3-1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}s.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-x+y$ inside the square defined in part 4.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}_{\\text{ave}}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386530 1113 #uuid "1b790b39-2d5a-502f-8a9f-500e59fc2b54" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386530 1145 "The interior of the square defined in the previous part can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\,:\\,-4<x<4,\\,-4<y<4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(R)=8\\cdot 8=64.\n$$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(R)}\\,{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}{∬}_{R}(x{y}^{3}-x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(x{y}^{3}-x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}-xy+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=-4}^{y=4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}[(64x-4x+8)-(64x+4x+8)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(-8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{-4}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}(16-16) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386531 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386532 13194139536158 true] [17592186386531 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536158 true] [17592186386531 1041 "field-1" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386531 1104 #edb/ref 17592186386532 13194139536158 true] [17592186386531 1110 #uuid "9fc9c2b7-afa1-506d-bff1-fa2453baa91f" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386532 1034 "0" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386532 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536158 true] [17592186386532 1111 #uuid "7ef1be8e-7b6c-51a7-86c1-dc6f24419d26" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386533 1017 #edb/ref 17592186386534 13194139536158 true] [17592186386533 1089 "q-168605" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386533 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536158 true] [17592186386533 1100 "An object generates an electric field given by $F(x,y)=(a{y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j$ in a planar coordinate system, where $a$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nGiven that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $6xy$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x,y)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $xy^3-x+y$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $+\\,C,$\nwhere $C$ is a constant-\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/assets/642a546fc498dfb0f01c0729959f9f68b8dc2d142d966673f204eda910f19211.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Consider the square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ shown above. The flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}^{}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $y^3-1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}s.$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-x+y$ inside the square defined in part 4.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}_{\\text{ave}}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/assets/6fd34e9070cd9555ba790e7300bb78b4d19f1b71b231e8a92f4e8f306c3d4623.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The region $D$ in the first quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above. \nIn polar coordinates, the following integral can be written as\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386533 1113 #uuid "29de82ee-c9ee-5673-b1fc-4f060a92a759" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386533 1145 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the disk enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386534 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386535 13194139536158 true] [17592186386534 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536158 true] [17592186386534 1041 "field-1" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386534 1104 #edb/ref 17592186386535 13194139536158 true] [17592186386534 1110 #uuid "afa6af64-7001-557f-b9d7-5d8ee051796c" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386535 1034 "r^2\\cos(\\theta)+r^2\\sin(\\theta)" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386535 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536158 true] [17592186386535 1111 #uuid "a651bcd8-98fe-5baa-9690-6a09742d0356" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386536 1017 #edb/ref 17592186386537 13194139536158 true] [17592186386536 1089 "q-168606" 13194139536158 true] [17592186386536 1091 #edb/ref 17592186